So a while ago I decided I'd take a username on NAO and ascend every
class/race combo.  That was over three months ago.  I started writing
this when I finally finished the last of the wizards (started on
valks).  It was the oddest ascension I've had.

As an aside, writing this has made me realize how long this game
really takes. I finished this game back in December and only now with
almost all of Jan gone, have i finished writing the summary, and I
skipped a lot of the less interesting battles..  Oh an I've also
ascended two Valks in the mean time.

Anyway here's the story of Comptril (Wiz-Hum-Mal-Neu)
The newly trained evoker entered the dungeon with his faithful
kitten.  Woefully unprepared, he only knew two spells, force bolt and
wizard lock.  A =oFA and a magic marker were the only items of note
that he had.

He's barely looked around a room or two when a filthy goblin attacks.
His quarterstaff is more than a match for the goblin tho and our hero
proceeds on his way.  Dlvl 1 has some not really notable stuff and he
climes down to dlvl2.

Less than 10 spaces from the stairs he finds a +oSleep.  This is one
of the more useful spells early on in the game.  So we're overjoyed to
find it. (Un)fortunately the rest of the level is devoid of anything.

Dlvl3 has a spellbook too difficult to understand. It's also the lvl
he leaves kitten on.  Dlvl4 our hero find a key, one of the most
essential items in the game (unless you have the knock spell or a /
oO).  4 is also the lvl he realized that he should have packed some
rations.  Hunger set in.  Luckily he did have a lichen to eat.
Shortly after that he found the entrance to the mines.

Now I've noticed the consensus (although it's not much of one) seems
to be that one should do Soko first then the Mines.  I use to agree
with this idea too, but no longer.  At least for wizards who can eat
Gnomes, I think the first level or two of the mines should be done and
a feast of Gnomes should be had.  Then continue to Soko, collect the
rations and the BoH or "oR, then return to finish the remainder of the
mines.

Another thing great about doing the first level of the mines is that
you get up to Xp:6 or so  rather quickly.  This is especially
important for wizards since they need the hp boost.  Finally you can
usually get a pick-axe from the first level of the mines.  Having this
essential tool early in the game can be very nice.

So this is what I had planned for our hero.  Our hero finished
exploring dlvl4 and headed into the Mines.  It was touch and go for a
while.  The first several dwarves and gnomes didn't leave any
corpses.  He was so very weak from hunger.  However the mines were lit
and finding the inhabitants was easy.  Soon he was no longer hungry.

Then an orc horde appeared.  No worries they're all in front of him.
Oh wait there are a bunch behind him too.  @#$@#$#@   uh-oh  down to
4hp.  Well I've never played an atheist before so why start now.  We
make him #pray away.   After that our hero has enough hp to be able to
survive the couple of hits he takes to flee the area.

There was a rolling boulder trap which, he saw and avoided. However
the orcs weren't as bright.  While our hero went off to whack some
more tasty gnomes, the orcs all fell victim to the rumbling of the
boulder.  How can orcs be really _that_ stupid?

The rest of the level had a few assorted items, but nothing worth
mentioning.  While our hero had some tasty snacks, he found himself
weak from hunger by the time he cleared the level..  So instead of
heading back up to the main branch he did the next level of the mines.

The 2nd level of the mines (dlvl6) went a little better than the
first.  It was also lit.  The downstairs from this level led to
minetown. Hopefully the mine branch will be short.   Our hero leveled
up to Xp:7 (the tan spellbook was still difficult to read).   He found
a brass lantern (not the greatest light source, but useful if darkness
is encountered).  And finally he was satiated from all the tasty
snacks.  However there were two areas that required some digging to
reach and he didn't have a pick-axe.

Since it looked like the branch might be short and our hero still had
not found a pick-axe, I decided to send him down into minetown.  He
found the downstairs before entering the town.  Since it was lit and
looked juicy he explored the 4th level of the mines (dlvl 8).  Ah
finally a pick-axe.  It was kinda heavy so this became a stash level
for his spellbooks.  Back up to the 2nd mine level to reach those two
inaccessible areas. Xp:8 with the help of the sleep ray.  Then he
found a bag!

Back to minetown.  The alter had an evil chaotic priest guarding it.
Also they must have been going through a recession or something as
there seemed to be a lot fewer shops than normal.  Hunger continued to
be a problem (usually by now it isn't a problem, I'm not sure what was
going on).  The few shops had little in the way of rations.

Then back down to the next level (dlvl8).  He cleared that with little
trouble and then finally was able to read that tan spellbook.  It was
cure sickness, a spell I find pretty useless after getting a blessed
unicorn horn.

Right after that, our hero encountered a spider.  After eating it he
felt healthy. Then before our hero had a chance to rest a band of
elves appeared.  Fortunately one got caught in a bear trap which
trapped one behind him.  This left just the leader who was dealt with
quickly. The other two were just pounded with force bolts.  Tasty
elves also made our hero feel wide awake.  Now the sleep ray was no
longer a threat and could be used with impunity!  Muhahahaha.

That was just in time as a soldier ants raiding party appeared on the
other side of the bear trap.  Our hero put them to sleep and fled back
to minetown to restore some Pw. Upon return the ants had got past the
bear trap, but the sleep ray almost took care of them.  One did wake
up while pounding it and took our hero down to 19hp.  However another
sleep ray + force bolt eliminated them.

Just when we were thinking the level was safe, a gargoyle appears.
Now I don't think I have many deaths by gargoyles, but that's b/c I
know to have the character run or engrave as soon as I spot one.  So
back to mine town for a rest.

Unfortunately it really seemed like touch and go in minetown.  Our
hero was out of Pw and hovering around 20 hp for much of the time.
Then he tamed a horse and started living on a diet of hill orcs.
Unfortunately a black light freaked him out and he started thinking
his horse was a Christmas Tree Monster, then something really nasty
took him down to 6hp.  So once again our hero called upon Thoth to
save him.  This worked and soon he was up to Xp:9.

After it was clear he wasn't going to get any rest in minetown, our
hero took his horse down to face the gargoyle.  The stupid horse was
completely useless and didn't attack.  However, a horse that doesn't
attack + Sleep ray + quarterstaff still equals easy victory.

He rests up for the next level and another gargoyle appears.  This
time it was a little more difficult, but our hero had a tasty snack of
gargoyle before the next level.   Ready for the next level our hero
discovers he's carrying a magic whistle.  A very good thing even if
the horse is useless.

The fifth level of the mines (dlvl9) was the first dark level.  The
horse took out the gnomes and our hero gorged on the feast. He also
tamed a kitten and found a /oDigging on this level!  Finally the E-
word can be engraved without fear. The wand arrived just in time since
he needed something to engrave with while dealing with a xan.

After dispatching the xan, our hero found a frost horn! Now I'm not
really sure what good the frost and fire horns are.  To me they're
basically like a heavy wand.  Maybe if I ever go in through the front
of the castle, I'd appreciate having a horn.  Since I almost always
use the back door, I never seem to have much use for horns (and I
never seem to find a tooled horn to deal with engulfers).  Anyway I
keep it in my inventory just in case I ever figure out the right way
to use it.

Our hero has a horrible scare as he sees a lich!  However this lich is
peaceful.  Oh my, a polymorph trap must be around somewhere.  Oh yes
there is the kitten seems to be missign and the horse has turned into
a lemur.

Oh well onward!  The next level is also dark.  It fast becomes the
level where he realizes the hunger problem is for the moment solved
and the opposite problem of over eating is starting.  Here's also
where he tests that bag to see if it is a BoH.  It's not :(  So this
level becomes another stash level.  Our hero has the lemur test a pair
of mud boots for curses, then strikes out in our new pair of boots of
speed!  WooHoo!

The 7th level of the mines (dlvl11) was lit.  At this point I'm
thinking the mines are a lot deeper than expected and we could
certainly use a BoH from Soko.  Of course our hero steps on a trap
door and ends up on the 9th level of the mines (dlvl13), which is
quite dark.  It's also quite catacomb like.  Ah finally mine's end!

This proved to be a fairly difficult level.  I haven't yet figured a
quick and easy way to kill M's with just force bolts and a
quarterstaff.  Also wargs and V's are quite difficult with just
these.  Of course wouldn't you know it, a pack of wargs and some V's
seemed to inhabit this level.  But fortunately the E-word works well
against them!

The problem of course is that while resting on the E-word in order to
charge up Pw, lots and lots of nasties spawn meaning we need even more
Pw, which takes longer to rest, which means lots more nasties spawn,
which means we need even more Pw, which takes longer to rest, which
more lots more nasties spawn....Ok you get the picture of the out-of-
control spiral of doom.   Ogres, ants, and many others joined with the
pack of wargs and V's.

Anyway we finally must have started making progress against the V as
he read a ?oEarth and brought the ceiling down.  Soon he was dust and
progress started on the rest of the horde.  We find a +oCreateFamilar
which is impossible to cast, then the lanter runs out.  Now no
problem, our hero has a !oOil.  #@$@#$@$ well aparently lanterns run
on something other than oil.  I never knew this and I wasn't very
happy to discover it now.

So now our hero is in the catacombs with no light and nasties lurking
about.  Not good.  However he does find the two gray stones and tames
a kitten.  However in short order, kitten gets taken out by an giant
zombie, while our hero suddenly finds himself back down to 13hp.

Somewhere in all this madness, he reached Xp:10.  He also got some
mental acquity. The first leqrotta appears.  A winged gargoyle
appears.  Our hero slowly make his way now to the center of the
catacombs.  It's a tough tedious process with the winged gargoyle
harassing him at every turn.  Our hero clears out the central room and
snags two spellbooks.  However the winged gargoyle attacks while he's
burdened with the books.  I really hate it when your hp is too low to
survive another round but too high to #pray.  This was certainly the
situation our hero was in.  Luckily he still had the /oDigging and
engraved the E-word.  Followed up with sleep and the quarterstaff
which finally rid him of the winged gargoyle menace.

The spellbooks were protection (I find this spell to be not really
useful as it runs out way too fast) and identify.  The latter is
perhaps the 3rd most useful spell after magic missile and finger of
death.  Now I was ecstatic, until I realized it was close to a 60%
failure rate!

Oh well just needs a little practice.  However Pw was a significant
problem.  Without the Eye Pw regeneration is quite slow.  This is why
wizards are so weak and pitiful in the beginning.  After ID'ing a few
key items (flintstone, oil skin sack), our hero heads up to the
unexplored 6th level of the mines (dlvl12).

It's another dark level and the lantern still won't accept the potion
of oil.  He does find another bag just in time for a black light to
freak him out.  This isn't a problem until he starts encountering
borgs and black dragons.  A "giant slug" takes our hero down to 8hp
and once again Thoth has to save our hero.  After that our hero flees
up to the next level, which is another basically uncleared level.

It turns out to be another tedious engrave and run level.  Our hero
did find a lamp which he swapped out for the dead lantern.  However
picking up some rations made him burdened, so this also became a stash
level.  Finally clearing out that level, our hero returns to the level
below.  Turns out all those scary things were just gnomes and dwarves.
Cleared it out and headed back up.

On the way back up he encountered a warhorse at the stairs.  Our hero
decided he didn't want another pet, but had to flee back down.  While
resting a black naga showed up.  With low hp and no pw: the only thing
that saved us was the frost horn.  But again it was more like a fancy /
oCold.

Our hero eventually returns and takes out the warhorse.  He proceeds
upward.  Back at minetown his ID spell does ID the entire inventory!
He apparently also have a fire horn to go with our frost horn!  A
couple of oil lamps and not too much else of interest.   After buying
7 candles from one of the evil greedy shopkeepers, our hero gives up
on minetown and heads up.

Back in the main branch the next level is dlvl5.  From just the layout
of the first few tunnels, I think this is the oracle.  Now I don't
know how the code works, but this level is always the hardest level
for my wizards.  After the above close calls I'm really hoping to get
him past quickly and into Soko.

Our hero tames a puppy just in time to confront a werebeast.  Hmmm it
was too easy.  Picking up random junk makes this another stash level.
He finally finds the hidden door to the central chamber.  It is the
oracle!  The statues contained only a +oRemoveCurse, which is a pretty
handy spell to have.  Then our hero leaves the puppy dog of doom in
Delphi and went looking for Soko.

On dlvl6 he finds a shop and reached Xp:11!  The shop was
uninteresting except for a plain spellbook.  Ya know with that magic
marker he started with he could have something really cool.
Hmmmmm...let's try it.  "My Diary" ooops that's not right.  "My Diary"
double oooops.  Tripple oops.   Ok give up that idea.  woah the book
is heavy.  Drop it and move on.

Entrance to Soko is found.  The rest of the level was uneventful.  I
send our hero back to the spellbock for another try.  The spellbook
warps strangely, then turns copper.  WooHoo!  Now to read it....

You feel a wrenching sensation.  You can move again.

Gah! It's cursed!  No worries we've got the remove curse spell.  @#$@#
$ That isn't working on the inventory just.  Our hero tries casting it
100 times but he never gets any better at it..

Upward he goes to the bottom of Soko (dlvl5).  The level becomes
another stash level.  I then get the bright idea that since protection
is clerical that I'll have him cast it 100s of time to improve remove
curse.

While waiting for Pw to recover our hero pushes some boulders around.
Soko is pretty devoid of food for Soko.  By the 2nd level (dlvl4) our
hero is hungry.  Of course maybe that's from casting protection a
lot.  Then the oops comes.  A boulder is pushed into the wrong
position.  Then another.  Now one will have to be force bolted. Well
maybe not.  But it's an ugly solution.

Shortly after this our hero gets better at clerical spells.  But still
not skilled enough that remove curse will work on items in the
inventory.  So push more boulders while waiting for Pw to recover.
Noooooooooooooooooooo a monster is trapped behind a boulder and we
don't have anything to attack it with!

So now we have him focus on improving clerical spells.  Lots of
waiting and casting protection.  Lots of hunger and our ration pile
grows even smaller.
While waiting a peaceful dog wanders around us.  Then right behind it
an orc horde.  Our hero tosses the dog a tripe ration to tame it.  But
the dog doesn't tame.  I've never seen this happen before.  Something
but be horribly wrong.  However it doesn't really matter the orcs are
horrible shots with their bows and arrows and kill the dog instead of
hitting our hero.   Soon the orcs were out of the way and our hero had
plenty of weapons to bombard the monster behind the boulder.

Our hero completes the boulders of the 2nd level of Soko.  Again he
waits to improve clerical spells before opening the door.  Good thing
he did since a yellow light seaped under the door.  While blind our
hero saw that almost the whole room was full of nasties.

So finally after what seems like forever, our hero knows clerical
spells well enough to remove curse from items in our inventor.  Remove
curse.  Read +oMM.  Wait for Pw and then charge!

MM is such a great spell.  It takes out the whole room so quickly.
Then wait to regain Pw before heading for the 3rd level of Soko.

The 3rd level also saw a boulder misplacement.  I don't know what was
wrong with me.  Two levels with wrong boulders.  At most I'll screw up
one level.  Usually I have no problem tho.  At least this last one
wasn't horrible.

But then I screwed up again.  And it was horrible. No choice but to
force bolt one of them or use a ?oEarth.  BAM the boulder shatters and
our hero completes that level.  No monsters at all in the room.

Getting to the zoo on Soko4 (dlvl2) proved to be quite easy.  No screw
ups.  No trapped monsters.  Just a flawless execution of the plan.
The zoo was stocked full of nasties.  If wasn't for judicious use of
MM and taming a puppy there would have been serious problems. Once the
zoo is finally cleared our hero grabs the all important BoH!!

Overjoyed once again he heads back down to the main dungeon. On the
way down he encounters a disenchanter.  Now frankly folks I fear these
more than MMF or ML. The time and trouble it takes to reenchant is
much more than the time it takes to remap with magic mapping or reID
with identify or jusk kill hordes of monsters with a =oConflict.  You
have to first find some blank scrolls and a magic marker or be able to
poly some scrolls into the right enchant scroll.  Overall
Disenchanters are more terrifying than any other beast in the dungeon
(ok that's an overstatement, but still they're right up there at the
top of the list of things that should be genocided).

But fortunately it turned out that it was only a chameleon.  This was
wonderful when our hero's puppy dog of doom ate it and suddenly became
the baby yellow dragon of doom!

Anyway back to the stash on the lowest soko level and ID some stuff.
He has a =oLevitation (very useful when you don't have boots or the
spell and don't want to risk a potion running out at an inopportune
time) and a =oTC.  Now TC + teleportitis is one of the most important
things you can get.  So I'm once again overjoyed by this discovery.

And down he goes with the baby dragon leaving only a few items in the
stash.  At this point both the mines and Soko are done.  My
probability of success just went from less than 5% to about 80%.
Although I lack almost all of the AK, I have a pretty good feeling.

Our hero clears dlvl7. There's a rare ice box with no useful food in
it.   There's a sink and he attracts a black pudding.  He kills it and
stores the corpse in the ice box for possible future use (pudding
farming is a lot easier than a sac fest with the create monster
spell).

There was also the dickwasher.  She wasn't very educational tho.  And
the =oPoly was pretty useless.  So on to dlvl8 and here was a white
unicorn.  Our hero took its horn with little effort.

Down again to dlvl9.  Not much to report there.  Dlvl 10 was the Big
Room. It was a Type III room (the one with the two structures in
middle). Baby dragon plus =oLevitiation + a /oDigging + the E-word
equals a Big Room that is quite easy.  MM also helped a lot.  Our hero
did have to flee up once, but this worked out really well as he tamed
a horse to join the dragon.  Unfortunately the horse didn't last too
long.

Nothing else of interest in the Big Room, so onward to dlvl11.  A very
small leprechaun hall here.  He tried and tried to get teleportitis
and finally felt jumpy.

Dlvl12 he gets the message to look for the IC transporter.  At this
point he's only Xp:11 so it's not really a big deal.  Must level up
first.  He does find the portal and make a brief visit to Nefret. Of
course Nefret sends him away to level up.

So down to dlvl 13.  Nothing to see move along.  Although I was sure
there was another room behind a secret passage, but he couldn't find
the door to it.

dlvl14 - a shop with lots o mimics and a t-shirt.  Somewhere in the
last two or three levels our hero lost the dragon.  I don't know where
or how but the dragon was just gone.

dlvl15 - the rogue level with Michael Toy's ghost.  At this point it's
fast approaching 20k turns.  However the only spells I feel he needs
to renew (MM & Id), he's got their spellbooks and they don't' actually
need renewing yet.

dlvl16 - yet another dungeon level.  This became yet another stash
level. He reall need to get that BoH blessed.  Rotten Wraith still at
Xp:12.

dlvl17- another lep hall, this time much bigger.  A pack of coutrals
which he had to flee up from. Unfortunately an ettin had spawned right
next to the downstairs on dlvl16 so he had to flee all the way up to
dlvl14 (not going to rest on the rogue level where there is no chance
of corpse generation for food).

Fortunately this setback didn't last too long.  Our hero used this
time to ID the stuff in the shop and read some ?oEA and ?oEW.  There
was also a +oCharmMoster and a /oCanc.  All very useful things. AC was
down to 3 and he also got ID down to 0% failure rate.  His
quarterstaff was now an astounding +2, if it could protect against
curses I'd have him keep it for the ascension run (just for style
points).

Unfortunately paying the shopkeeper meant that he let all sorts of
nasties in before our hero had enough time to regen Pw to cast charm
monster.  Things were looking really bad when a sergeant didn't flee
from the E-word.  Only thing that saved him was teleportities kicked
and he teleported to the stairs.  He tamed a horse there and watied
for the mob to come.  While the mob did slowly come, they for the most
part fell victim to a level teleport trap leaving our hero much safer
than he'd otherwise be. However, it was still pretty slow going.  The
horse died and the monsters kept coming before our hero was fully
rested.

I often wonder when I encounter a level like this if it is better to
flee (either up or down) just to get off the level.  It usually works
well when I do this on the Oracle level, but the success rate on other
levels seems mixed.  So at least this one we kept hanging out on
dlvl14.

Finally at one point our hero's Pw was just high enough to attempt
charm monster.  He got a pet frost giant.  Muhahaha! Time to kill
those vile greedy shopkeepers!

Just then a nurse appears.  Now I normally don't use the nurses much.
However this time it seemed necessary.  AC was only 3. Our hero was
down on dlvl14 and still didn't have Magicbane.  Pets seemed to be
dying at a much higher rate than normal so more HP sounded like a very
good thing and well worth the exorbitant amount of time it takes to
get even 1hp.

The frost giant did a pretty good job of keeping the nasties away
while the nurse worked on our hero's nakkid body.  Unfortunately our
frost giant didn't quite understand what the nurse was doing and ended
the treatment.

If the frost giant was going to kill people, why not have him kill
people we want to kill?  A quick visit to the greedy shopkeeper.
Unfortunately the frost giant wouldn't attack.  Oh well lets take him
down to the coutrals.

First he had to dispatch the ettin on dlvl16.  The frost giant took
care of that with ease.  He also handled the coutrals as well.

dlvl17 had a beehive.  It was pretty easy.  Unfortunately only about 3
lumps of royal jelley in this huge hive.  Our hero charmed a fire
giant.  And finally the room with the downstairs was devishly hidden.
Our hero had to make a hole and come back up to find the stairs.

Our hero ended up on dlvl19.  Found the downstairs and made a stash on
dlvl20. Then decided that was a bad idea and figured going around
burdened wouldn't' be that horrible.  Then I recalled how many times
I've killed chars b/c they were burdened and started unloading on
dlvl19.  Our hero fell through a trap door back to dlvl20 and fled up
only to encountere an elf king.

dlvl19 had a magic shop.  He bought a /oMakeInvi and some ?oEA.
Invisibility is awesome, I'll even take it in the early game when its
disadvantages are quite apparent.  However we're past the point where
we'll be needing to visit shops much.  So zap away!  Now he's down to
AC:2 and the quarterstaff is at +3! Now I'm really feeling good about
the way things are going.

Onward.  Our hero finally found the secret room on dlv18 by going up
from dlvl19.  While waiting for Pw to recover to fight the
baluchitherium guarding that room, he ate the royal jelly and got St:
18/04.  When he finally got up there and defeated the q,  the frost
giant is the only thing still tame.

Back to the shop on dlvl19 and hopefully the frost giant will do its
thing this time.  Yea it does.  It also helps take out the elf king on
dlvl20.  Our hero finds a small barracks as well before moving.
Dlvl21 also had a barracks slightly larger than the previous one. It
was also quite a bit more difficult.  Unfortunately neither of them
had anything of use.  Also unfortunately, frosty the snow giant got
taken out by a /oFire on one of the soldiers.

dlvl22 had yet another lep hall, slightly larger than the previous
ones.  One of the little green l's had a /oDigging and our hero went
crashing down to dlvl24.  He headed immediately up to dlvl23 and then
back to dlvl22 and finished the leps.

There was also a note about to the vault, although he didn't look for
it.  Back on 23 he found a swamp and was almost killed by a giant eel.
Fortunately MM took care of it and he fled the room.  Our hero then
tracked down the lep with the wand and fell down another one of his
holes to dlvl24.  He escaped as our hero found a zoo on the level.

Our hero had to flee from the zoo, but explored the rest of the
level.  A nice water troll fell victim to his charm spell.  A black
unicorn had a !oGL.  Unfortunately it was cursed, but I figured that
would be good for leaving the dungeon with the amulet.  Then back to
the zoo, where he found a wraith.  Xp:14!  Now he can go back and do
